/-!
# Analytic sets in the sense of a paved space

A paving of `𝓧` is a set of sets `p : Set (Set 𝓧)`. A set `s` is analytic for `p` if it is the
projection on `𝓧` of a set of `𝓧 × 𝓚` that is a countable intersection of countable unions of
products `A ×ˢ K` with `A ∈ p` and `K` in a compact system of `𝓚`.

## Main definitions

* `MeasureTheory.IsPavingAnalyticFor p 𝓚 s`: `s` is analytic for the paving `p`, with an auxiliary
  space `𝓚`.
* `MeasureTheory.IsPavingAnalytic p s`: `s` is analytic for the paving `p`.
* `MeasureTheory.IsMeasurableAnalytic s`: `s` is the projection of a measurable set of `𝓧 × ℝ`.

## Main statements

* `MeasureTheory.IsPavingAnalytic.iUnion`, `MeasureTheory.IsPavingAnalytic.iInter`: analytic sets
  are stable by countable unions and intersections.
* `MeasureTheory.IsPavingAnalytic.fst`: the projection of an analytic set for a product paving
  `p × q`, with `q` a compact system, is analytic for `p`.
* `MeasureTheory.isPavingAnalytic_isPavingAnalytic_iff`: a set analytic for the paving of analytic
  sets is analytic.
* `MeasureTheory.isPavingAnalytic_of_measurableSet_generateFrom`: if the complement of every set of
  `p` is analytic, then every set of the σ-algebra generated by `p` is analytic.
* `MeasurableSet.isPavingAnalytic_fst`: the projection of a measurable set of `𝓧 × ℝ` is analytic
  for the paving of measurable sets.

## Implementation notes

In the definition of `IsPavingAnalytic`, the auxiliary space `𝓚` lives in `Type`, since we cannot
quantify over universes.

Some references require the compact system `q` to contain `∅` and the auxiliary space `𝓚` to be
nonempty. This gives the same notion: `insert ∅ q` is a compact system if `q` is one, and a set
that is analytic with an empty auxiliary space is empty, hence analytic with auxiliary space `ℝ`.

We use `IsCompactSystem`, which corresponds to the semi-compact pavings of Dellacherie and Meyer,
rather than compact pavings (the same notion with arbitrary intersections instead of countable
ones): this is sufficient for our applications and easier to work with.

## References

* [K. Bichteler, *Stochastic integration with jumps*][bichteler2002stochastic]
* [S. He, J. Wang and J. Yan, *Semimartingale theory and stochastic calculus*][he2019semimartingale]
-/

@[expose] public section

open scoped ENNReal NNReal

variable {𝓧 𝓨 𝓚 𝓚' ι : Type*} {p : Set (Set 𝓧)} {q : Set (Set 𝓚)} {s t : Set 𝓧} {f : ℕ → Set 𝓧}

